  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 AM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nRegarding smoke potential, the HRRR shows that the northern tier of \nSouth Dakota and down into the Glacial Lakes region will have high \nsmoke through the day today, and less for Sunday. Thus today it \ncould play spoiler-maker in regards to highs across north \ncentral/northeast counties, but less so for central South Dakota, \nand no/limited impact to temperatures Sunday. As for near surface \nsmoke, its pretty limited to North Dakota, but north central South \nDakota may intermittently have lower visibility shift back and forth \nalong the border through the weekend. Generally the southerly flow \nacross the area should advect in smoke-free are until there is a \nboundary later Sunday so will need to re-evauate during the next \ncouple of longer HRRR runs on the day and tonight shifts for \npotential impacts to visibility/air quality. Most of this is coming \nin from the Pacific Northwest and typically by the time it arrives \nit has been fairly well mixed.  \n\nCAMS bring a smattering of shower/weak thunderstorm activity in \nacross the west during the late this afternoon/evening with a weak \nwave that crests the ridge. NAM BUFKIT profiles indicate that this \ncould be surface based on account of the very hot temperatures, but \nlikewise very high based, up around +12kft and fall just a few \nhundred feet below the freezing level. Skinny CAPE resides above a \ndeep dry layer, potentially leading to dry storms with dry \nmicroburst/heat bursts. Probably have to deal with some lightning \nstarts as well. \n\nMain focus remains the heat for the weekend. While heat index values \nat times are forecast above the 105 threshold for Extreme Heat \nWarning criteria, its only for a few locations and more broadly the \nHeat Advisory headlines covers conditions fairly well. Sunday is a \nlittle more questionable with a broad area of 105+ across central \nSouth Dakota, a few spots to 110. Overnight lows are also not a slam \ndunk needed for an upgrade as most locations are forecast into the \nlow 70s.\n\nWith a developing more traditional 4-Corners high, the next wave \nthat takes the circuitous route around the high across the \nintermountain west and ejects into the Northern Plains sometime \nMonday/Monday night. There are a few more showing up in the NBM \nthrough the week, though as we get late into the week/next weekend \nthe ridge continues to retrograde and we get into a more consistent \nnorthwesterly flow regime. This means a generally trend towards \ncloser to average temperatures albeit still hot with temperatures on \naverage next week about 5 to 15 degrees above normal. Still none of \nthose over the top ridiculous well into the 70s to near 80 degree \ndewpoints showing up in the NBM.",
